# ------------------------------------------------------------------ abstract
MS.append((
"""**Objective.** Osteoarthritis (OA) and bone mineral density (BMD) are clinically entangled: individuals with OA tend to have higher BMD, yet whether this reflects shared genetic aetiology, causal direction, or confounding remains unresolved. Prior cross-trait studies used few BMD sites, a single OA definition, or correlation alone without causal inference. We systematically quantified genetic sharing and causal direction across 13 skeletal phenotypes.""",
"""**Objective.** People with osteoarthritis (OA) tend to have higher bone mineral density (BMD), but the basis of this association is unsettled: it may reflect shared genetic aetiology, a causal effect in one direction or the other, or confounding. Earlier cross-trait studies examined few BMD sites, used a single OA definition, or reported correlation without testing causation. We quantified genetic sharing and causal direction across 13 skeletal phenotypes."""))

MS.append((
"""**Results.** LDSC recovered a coherent three-block structure: BMD sites were highly inter-correlated (r_g=0.69–0.96; all q<0.001), OA was moderately and consistently positively correlated with all ten BMD sites (r_g=0.12–0.25; all q<0.001), and the two OA definitions were strongly correlated (r_g=+0.84). Fracture was negatively correlated with every BMD site (r_g=−0.45 to −0.60; all q<0.001), a canonical relationship serving as a biological validation of the pipeline. MR confirmed the positive control (BMD→fracture β=−0.337 per SD, OR 1.40, p=3.7×10⁻⁵¹) and showed that genetically predicted BMD causally *increased* knee OA risk (β=+0.02 to +0.03 per SD across five sites; p(IVW)<1×10⁻⁴; no directional pleiotropy by MR-Egger intercept, all p>0.24). Reverse MR (OA→BMD) and OA→fracture were null.""",
"""**Results.** LDSC resolved three blocks. The ten BMD sites were strongly inter-correlated (r_g 0.69–0.96; all q<0.001); both OA definitions correlated positively with every BMD site (r_g 0.12–0.25; all q<0.001); and the two OA definitions correlated with each other at r_g=+0.84. Fracture correlated negatively with every BMD site (r_g −0.45 to −0.60; all q<0.001), reproducing the established inverse BMD–fracture relationship. MR reproduced the positive control (BMD→fracture β=−0.337 per SD, OR 1.40, p=3.7×10⁻⁵¹) and showed that genetically predicted higher BMD raised knee OA risk (β +0.02 to +0.03 per SD across five sites; p_IVW <1×10⁻⁴; MR-Egger intercepts all p>0.24). Reverse MR (OA→BMD) and OA→fracture were null."""))

MS.append((
"""**Conclusion.** Higher BMD is genetically protective against fracture but causally increases OA risk, and OA does not causally elevate BMD. These findings reconcile the OA–BMD paradox, indicate that OA's genetic link to BMD is not a pathway to fracture, and suggest that interventions increasing BMD should be evaluated for skeletal-site–specific OA consequences.""",
"""**Conclusion.** Higher BMD protects against fracture but raises OA risk, and OA does not raise BMD in turn. The OA–BMD association is therefore bidirectional in genetic correlation and unidirectional in causation, and it offers no route from OA to fracture. Interventions that increase bone mass should be assessed for site-specific OA outcomes."""))

# -------------------------------------------------------------- introduction
MS.append((
"""Osteoarthritis (OA) and bone mineral density (BMD) are the two most prevalent skeletal traits in ageing populations, and their epidemiological relationship has been contested for five decades. Foss and Byers first reported elevated femoral-head BMD in osteoarthritic hips in 1972 [REF]. Since then, observational studies have repeatedly found that higher areal BMD is *associated with increased* OA prevalence and incidence, while osteoporosis and OA were long considered inversely related [REF]. The direction of this association matters clinically: if high BMD truly predisposes to OA, then anti-osteoporotic strategies that increase bone mass may carry an OA cost; if, conversely, OA raises BMD through altered loading, the interpretation is entirely different.""",
"""Osteoarthritis (OA) and bone mineral density (BMD) are the two most prevalent skeletal traits in ageing populations, and the nature of their epidemiological association has been debated since Foss and Byers reported raised femoral-head BMD in osteoarthritic hips in 1972 [REF]. Observational studies have since linked higher areal BMD to increased OA prevalence and incidence, although osteoporosis and OA were long regarded as opposing conditions [REF]. The direction matters clinically. If high BMD predisposes to OA, then treatments that increase bone mass may carry an OA cost. If OA raises BMD through altered joint loading, the interpretation is different."""))

MS.append((
"""Genome-wide methods offer a route to disentangle this. Linkage disequilibrium score regression (LDSC) estimates genetic correlation (r_g) from summary statistics alone, is robust to sample overlap, and is unbiased by stratification when the LDSC intercept is near unity [Bulik-Sullivan 2015, REF]. Two-sample Mendelian randomisation (MR) then uses genetic variants as instruments to interrogate causality [REF]. Applied to OA and BMD, these approaches have yielded partial answers: Hackinger *et al.* reported r_g=+0.18 between hip/knee OA and lumbar-spine BMD (p=0.022) [Hackinger 2017 HMG, REF], and Zhou *et al.* reported r_g=+0.13 (LDSC) and +0.17 (HDL) between hip OA and femoral-neck BMD, with bidirectional MR supporting a negative causal effect of FN-BMD on hip OA [Zhou 2025 Front Genet, REF].""",
"""Genome-wide data can separate these explanations. Linkage disequilibrium score regression (LDSC) estimates genetic correlation (r_g) from summary statistics, tolerates sample overlap, and is unbiased by stratification when the intercept is near unity [Bulik-Sullivan 2015, REF]. Two-sample Mendelian randomisation (MR) uses genetic variants as instruments to interrogate causality [REF]. Applied to OA and BMD, both have given partial answers. Hackinger et al. reported r_g=+0.18 between hip/knee OA and lumbar-spine BMD (p=0.022) [Hackinger 2017 HMG, REF]; Zhou et al. reported r_g=+0.13 by LDSC and +0.17 by HDL between hip OA and femoral-neck BMD, with bidirectional MR supporting a negative causal effect of femoral-neck BMD on hip OA [Zhou 2025 Front Genet, REF]."""))

MS.append((
"""Three gaps remain. **First**, most analyses used at most two BMD sites; site specificity is unknown, even though OA is a joint-localised disease and BMD is site-specifically regulated. **Second**, OA has usually been represented by a single definition; whether clinically ascertained radiographic OA and community self-reported OA share the same genetic relationship with bone is unclear. **Third**, the OA–BMD–fracture triangle has not been analysed jointly: given that fracture is the canonical *negative* correlate of BMD, simultaneously modelling OA, BMD and fracture in one framework both validates the pipeline and asks whether OA's BMD association has any fracture relevance.""",
"""Three questions remain open. First, most analyses included at most two BMD sites, so site specificity is unknown, even though OA is joint-localised and BMD is regulated site by site. Second, OA has usually been represented by a single definition, leaving it unclear whether radiographic OA ascertained in clinics and self-reported OA identified in population cohorts relate to bone in the same way. Third, the OA–BMD–fracture triangle has not been analysed jointly. Because fracture is the established negative correlate of BMD, modelling OA, BMD and fracture together tests the pipeline and asks whether the OA–BMD association carries any fracture relevance."""))

MS.append((
"""We addressed all three by analysing **13 skeletal phenotypes** — two OA definitions, ten regional BMD sites, and any-fracture — with (i) LDSC across all 78 phenotype pairs, (ii) two-sample MR for causal direction with a built-in positive control, and (iii) reverse MR to discriminate BMD→OA from OA→BMD.""",
"""We addressed all three in 13 skeletal phenotypes (two OA definitions, ten regional BMD sites, and any-fracture), using LDSC across all 78 phenotype pairs, two-sample MR for causal direction with an internal positive control, and reverse MR to distinguish BMD→OA from OA→BMD."""))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------- methods
MS.append((
"""**Provenance and sample sizes:** `OA_knee` — Genetics of Osteoarthritis (GO) Consortium 2025 (*Nature* 641:1217–1224; doi:10.1038/s41586-025-08771-z; PMID 40205036), knee-OA proband subset N=1,208,397 (within the GO Consortium European OA meta-analysis, whose full European sample was ≈1.32M); the ten BMD sites — Qian et al. 2025 (*Genomics Proteomics Bioinformatics*; doi:10.1093/gpbjnl/qzaf097, PMID 41206123), DXA-derived BMD at 11 skeletal sites in European UK Biobank participants, with verified per-site accession numbers and sample sizes in the GWAS Catalog series GCST90568448–58: lumbar spine GCST90568448 (N=30,449), legs GCST90568451 (N=31,873), pelvis GCST90568452 (N=31,873), total body GCST90568455 (N=31,986), trunk GCST90568456 (N=31,986), femur total GCST90568457 (N=31,873), femoral neck GCST90568458 (N=32,017); all N verified directly in the GWAS Catalog — an independent back-calculation of N from the top-hit standard errors gave ≈29.7k, within ~7% of the reported values (the small difference is absorbed by the GEFOS validation in §3.7); `OA_selfrep` — self-reported OA from the IEU OpenGWAS resource (`ukb-b-14486`; MRC-IEU / Ben Elsworth 2018), N=462,933 (38,472 cases / 424,461 controls); `fracture` — GEFOS consortium 2018 (Trajanoska et al., *BMJ* k3225, PMID 30158200), any-fracture N=264,973 (37,857 cases / 227,116 controls). Full details and accession numbers are in the Data Availability statement and References (see also `docs/10_参考文献与原始GWAS来源.md`); all BMD accession numbers and per-site sample sizes were verified directly in the GWAS Catalog.""",
"""Sources and sample sizes were as follows. `OA_knee` came from the Genetics of Osteoarthritis (GO) Consortium 2025 (*Nature* 641:1217–1224; doi:10.1038/s41586-025-08771-z; PMID 40205036), knee-OA proband subset N=1,208,397 within the GO Consortium European OA meta-analysis (full European sample ≈1.32M). The ten BMD sites came from Qian et al. 2025 (*Genomics Proteomics Bioinformatics*; doi:10.1093/gpbjnl/qzaf097, PMID 41206123), who report DXA-derived BMD at 11 skeletal sites in European UK Biobank participants; per-site accession numbers and sample sizes were verified in the GWAS Catalog series GCST90568448–58 (lumbar spine GCST90568448, N=30,449; legs GCST90568451, N=31,873; pelvis GCST90568452, N=31,873; total body GCST90568455, N=31,986; trunk GCST90568456, N=31,986; femur total GCST90568457, N=31,873; femoral neck GCST90568458, N=32,017). Back-calculating N from the top-hit standard errors gave ≈29.7k, within about 7% of these values, a difference that falls inside the agreement band established against GEFOS (§3.7). `OA_selfrep` was self-reported OA from the IEU OpenGWAS resource (`ukb-b-14486`; MRC-IEU / Ben Elsworth 2018), N=462,933 (38,472 cases / 424,461 controls). `fracture` was any-fracture from the GEFOS consortium 2018 (Trajanoska et al., *BMJ* k3225, PMID 30158200), N=264,973 (37,857 cases / 227,116 controls). Full details and accession numbers are in the Data Availability statement and References (see also `docs/10_参考文献与原始GWAS来源.md`)."""))

MS.append((
"""Because cross-trait analyses are exquisitely sensitive to allele orientation, we performed two independent verifications. **(i) Biological validation.** Top loci matched known biology: rs224333/rs9930333 in *GDF5* and *FTO* for knee OA, rs7108738 in *SOX6* and rs3779381 in *WNT16* for femoral-neck BMD, each exhibiting contiguous multi-SNP LD blocks of same-direction effects, which random or synthetic data cannot produce. **(ii) Literature anchor.** Our estimated r_g(knee OA, lumbar-spine BMD) = +0.143 and r_g(knee OA, femoral-neck BMD) = +0.173 closely reproduce published values (+0.18 [Hackinger 2017]; +0.13 [Zhou 2025]), establishing the correct allele orientation.""",
"""Cross-trait analyses depend on correct allele orientation, so we checked it in two ways. First, the top loci matched known biology: rs224333 and rs9930333 in *GDF5* and *FTO* for knee OA, rs7108738 in *SOX6* and rs3779381 in *WNT16* for femoral-neck BMD, each forming contiguous multi-SNP LD blocks with effects in the same direction, a pattern that random or synthetic data would not produce. Second, our estimates of r_g(knee OA, lumbar-spine BMD) = +0.143 and r_g(knee OA, femoral-neck BMD) = +0.173 reproduce the published values (+0.18 [Hackinger 2017]; +0.13 [Zhou 2025])."""))

MS.append((
"""A **common variant set across all 13 phenotypes** (n = 690,150) was used so that every cell of the r_g matrix is estimated on the same SNPs, making the matrix internally comparable (the official tool uses pairwise intersections, which are not directly comparable across pairs). Palindromic SNPs (A/T, C/G) were excluded as strand-ambiguous. Alleles were harmonised by name against a single reference phenotype; SNPs whose alleles could not be matched in either orientation were dropped. A single dataset required a global Z-sign correction, which was applied and documented (§5 and the Supplement, `docs/11_补充材料_OA_selfrep_Z校正.md`).""",
"""All 13 phenotypes were analysed on a common set of 690,150 SNPs, so that every cell of the r_g matrix is estimated on the same variants and the cells can be compared with one another (the official tool uses pairwise intersections, which cannot). Palindromic SNPs (A/T, C/G) were excluded as strand-ambiguous. Alleles were harmonised by name against a single reference phenotype, and SNPs that could not be matched in either orientation were dropped. One dataset required a global Z-sign correction, which was applied and is documented in the Supplement (`docs/11_补充材料_OA_selfrep_Z校正.md`)."""))

MS.append((
"""Instruments were selected from each exposure GWAS at p < 5×10⁻⁸. Two instrument-selection strategies were evaluated: **strict clumping equivalent to `plink --clump --clump-p1 5e-8 --clump-p2 5e-8 --clump-r2 0.001 --clump-kb 10000`** (the primary analysis) against the 1000 Genomes Phase 3 European reference panel (1kg.v3; 503 EUR individuals, 8,550,156 variants, GRCh37), and the conservative 1-Mb window clumping (sensitivity analysis). Because a PLINK binary was not available in the analysis environment, clumping was re-implemented in Python by reading the PLINK 1 `.bed` bitstream directly (`code/18_plink_clump_pure.py`); the greedy algorithm and all thresholds are identical to PLINK's. Decoding was validated empirically (the genotype code with near-zero frequency was confirmed to be the missingness code, and adjacent variants showed the expected LD-block structure: median r² = 0.041 with 23% of pairs within 100 kb at r² > 0.8). Of the genome-wide-significant SNPs, 99.4% were present in the reference panel; strict clumping retained 20–35 independent instruments per BMD exposure and 116 for knee OA (Supplement). The **MR-PRESSO global (distortion) test** (Verbanck et al. 2018) was then applied to the strict instrument sets. Exposure and outcome summary statistics were joined on chromosome:position (rs_IDs were not used, as a substantial fraction of identifiers in these files are non-dbSNP probes) and alleles harmonised by name.""",
"""Instruments were selected from each exposure GWAS at p < 5×10⁻⁸. We compared two selection strategies against the 1000 Genomes Phase 3 European reference panel (1kg.v3; 503 EUR individuals, 8,550,156 variants, GRCh37). The primary analysis used strict clumping equivalent to `plink --clump --clump-p1 5e-8 --clump-p2 5e-8 --clump-r2 0.001 --clump-kb 10000`; a conservative 1-Mb window was used as a sensitivity analysis. No PLINK binary was available in the analysis environment, so clumping was re-implemented in Python by reading the PLINK 1 `.bed` bitstream directly (`code/18_plink_clump_pure.py`); the greedy algorithm and all thresholds match PLINK's. Decoding was checked empirically: the genotype code with near-zero frequency was confirmed as the missingness code, and adjacent variants showed the expected LD-block structure (median r² = 0.041, with 23% of pairs within 100 kb at r² > 0.8). Of the genome-wide-significant SNPs, 99.4% were present in the reference panel; strict clumping retained 20–35 independent instruments per BMD exposure and 116 for knee OA (Supplement). The MR-PRESSO global (distortion) test (Verbanck et al. 2018) was then applied to the strict instrument sets. Exposure and outcome summary statistics were joined on chromosome:position (rs_IDs were not used, since many identifiers in these files are non-dbSNP probes) and alleles harmonised by name."""))

